  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains the predominant feature for today and \ntonight as confluence aloft continues to build pressure through the \nlower troposphere. The center of this surface high is expected to \ntrack just to the north of central Indiana of which should lead to a \nlight easterly winds throughout the day. Low level cloud cover \nshould be fairly minimal throughout the day due to the ongoing \nsubsidence, but a few diurnal cumulus may be possible as upward \nlatent heat fluxes help provide some moisture to the PBL. Aloft, \nmoisture within an upstream mid level trough feature will likely be \nkicked into the jet stream, with scattered cirrus clouds expected \nover central Indiana by mid afternoon.  \n\n850mb temperatures will be the same today as yesterday, with \nsurface temperatures expected to respond accordingly; given the \nslightly drier near surface air, afternoon highs will likely push \na bit higher with the current expected highs between 84-86F. The \nonly hazard of concern for today and tonight will be some \npotential for fog development near the pressure center over N/NW \nportions of the area. A majority of the fog should be contained to\nlow lying valleys and well irregated farmland, but this may \nadvect elsewhere depending on depth of any fog layer. \n\nAs mentioned, a mid level trough feature will continue to develop \nthroughout the day, mostly due to ongoing super-geostrophic flow \nover the Rockies aiding in pressure falls over the western Missouri \nValley. Due to the placement of the jet, much of the pressure \ndepletion will be due to very strong diffluence and thus weak \nsteering flow with a majority of the ensemble members showing the \nmid level low becoming detached from the flow aloft. The initial \nrain threat is expected to be Friday late afternoon into the evening \nalong an elongated confluence band as the low pushes eastward. Any \nrain on Friday afternoon and evening should be mostly light, but a \nfew elevated convective cells will be possible leading to a larger \nrange in QPF outcomes. \n\nFor Friday night through Saturday, forecast confidence starts to \nwane. While ensemble members agree on the overall pattern, the path \nand the strength of the cutoff low have varied some over the last \nfew runs due to the lack of strong dynamics. Latest trends have \nshifted the center of the flow further north, nearing southern \nportions of the area on Saturday. Due to the weak steering flow but \nabundant upward lift near the cutoff low, near stationary and \ntraining convection may be possible at times Friday night through \nSaturday leading to an excessive rainfall threat over most of the \narea. While an isolated damaging wind gust cannot be ruled out \nthroughout this event, weak low level lapse rates and bulk shear \nwill limit the severity of most thunderstorm. The best chance for \nany severe wind gusts will be on the east to southeast side of the \nlow where the LLJ is a bit stronger, helping create bulk shear \nvalues closer to 30kt.  \n\nFor next week, there are not any strong signals within the 300mb \npattern leading to fairly high uncertainty on precipitation. \nHowever, regarding temperatures, the polar jet looks to be well \ndisplaced to the north, with should allow for above average 850mb \ntemperatures and therefor some likelihood of above average surface \ntemperatures.",
